ople thrown in there as well but the  one I want  highlight in terms of the supporting  cast is Shawn bigger staff playing a  character called Chris he basically  function he has the add-on companion for  just this this story in this adventure  and he is wonderful I mean he's just  awesome he's exactly what I love out of  a campaign because there's stucco and he  has no ideas way of his death but  something happens that he doesn't  understand he just kind of goes oh okay  and that's that's the kind of response  to the reaction that I always love from  a wood companion and he's great and he  some people I mean if you're a Harry  Potter fan you know Shawn bigger staff  has Oliver wood from the early Harry  Potter movies and he's great he was so  good in fact that I looked up whether  they actually you ever used him again as  a more ongoing companion for 8dr audio  adventures and sadly they don't how I  wish they did  feels like a waste he was really good  and he's got a great rapport with McGann  in terms of the rest of the supporting  cast all awareness fine John Leeson  comes back as k9 and he's good but I  mean can I can I can get over I think  they got the right dosage of him in this  one so he works just fine but honest of  Cronos is like I said felt familiar felt  almost like slipping into an old sweater  just getting that character again who I  already knew if there's a fall down on  this thing it's a couple of things the  big one though is the villain now the  villain in terms of their scheme it's  kind of generic but it's it comes down  more I think to both the writing and the  performance because one of the downsides  of adapting Douglas Adams a script from  the 1970s is that this feels like a very  70s era doctor whose story particularly  when it comes to the villain which is  just he's a very arch villain he's very  nice and which was kind of the  bog-standard one-off villain from that  era of Doctor Who and that's exactly  what we get here and it's just i